   $q$Read the sentences: "Ms. Okafor teaches eighth-grade science. She built a weather station on the school roof." Which choice combines them by using an appositive?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Teaching eighth-grade science, Ms. Okafor built a weather station on the school roof.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"participial-phrase-not-appositive"},{"text":"Ms. Okafor, an eighth-grade science teacher, built a weather station on the school roof.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Ms. Okafor teaches eighth-grade science, she built a weather station on the school roof.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-splice-created"},{"text":"Ms. Okafor, who teaches eighth-grade science, built a weather station on the school roof.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"relative-clause-not-appositive"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Ms. Okafor, an eighth-grade science teacher, built a weather station on the school roof.$q$, $q$An appositive is a noun phrase that renames a noun, and "an eighth-grade science teacher" renames Ms. Okafor. The "who teaches" version is correct but uses a relative clause, not an appositive.$q$, 'draft', null)
