  ('df14d72a-29a1-5f5e-9ba5-4917e2534f49', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g9-argument-writing'), null, 'hard',
   $q$A reader says paragraph two treats the board's concern too briefly. Which sentence, added after sentence (10), would best address this while keeping the writer's claim?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Visitors do deserve safe parking, so volunteers could mark the field and guide cars there on festival weekends.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Parking is a common problem at museums in many parts of the country.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-general"},{"text":"The board members have probably never even walked through the orchard.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"attacks-opponent"},{"text":"The museum could build the new lot on just half of the orchard instead.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"undercuts-claim"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Visitors do deserve safe parking, so volunteers could mark the field and guide cars there on festival weekends.$q$, $q$The correct sentence takes the board's safety goal seriously and shows how the overflow field would meet it, so the orchard can stay. Building on half the orchard would give up part of the writer's own claim.$q$, 'draft', 'c02e7959-c79e-51f0-b3d8-c97d30e469cc')
