insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('9aa3e835-3388-5d59-9d92-569aaddb4caa', 'wg9-informative-writing-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g9-informative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$How a Canal Lock Lifts a Boat$q$, $q$(1) Boats cannot sail uphill, yet a barge on the Calder Canal climbs nearly 60 meters on its trip between the river port of Wexley and the inland town of Brannock. (2) The barge makes this climb by passing through a series of canal locks. (3) A lock is a watertight chamber with gates at both ends that raises or lowers boats from one water level to another.

(4) The process begins when a boat heading upstream enters the chamber through the lower gates, which then close behind it. (5) Next, the lock keeper opens small valves, called paddles, in the upper gates. (6) Water from the higher section of the canal pours into the chamber, and the boat floats upward with it. (7) When the water inside matches the level outside, the upper gates swing open and the boat continues on its way.

(8) Locks need no engines or pumps to work. (9) Gravity moves all of the water, so the only energy needed is the effort of opening the gates and paddles. (10) Many of the gates on the Calder Canal are still pushed open by hand using long wooden beams.

(11) The canal was finished in 1798, when roads in the region were often too muddy for heavy wagons. (12) A single horse pulling a barge along the canal could move about 30 tons of coal, while the same horse could pull only about 2 tons on a road. (13) Coal is a black rock that formed over millions of years from the remains of ancient plants.

(14) Today, the Calder Canal carries mostly tour boats and kayaks, and its 22 locks still work.$q$, 'draft')
