insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('cbbbbbcc-5e85-59e4-8c34-7d2987c29364', 'wg9-informative-writing-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g9-informative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Why Curlers Sweep the Ice$q$, $q$(1) Curling is a winter sport. (2) Two teams take turns sliding heavy granite stones down a long sheet of ice toward a target painted at the far end, called the house. (3) After a player releases a stone, two teammates walk alongside it and sweep the ice in front of it with special brushes. (4) To someone watching for the first time, the sweepers might look as if they are cleaning, but their work actually changes the stone's path.

(5) The surface of a curling sheet is not perfectly smooth. (6) Before each game, workers sprinkle drops of water over the ice, which freeze into tiny bumps known as pebble. (7) A moving stone rides on top of these bumps. (8) When sweepers scrub the ice, the friction from their brushes warms the pebble slightly and creates a very thin layer of water. (9) The stone slides more easily over this layer, so it travels farther and curves less.

(10) Sweeping can make a large difference. (11) In tests at the Northfield Curling Club, hard sweeping added as much as 3 meters to a stone's slide. (12) Sweepers must communicate constantly with the player who threw the stone, who calls out whether to sweep harder or stop. (13) Curling stones are made of a rare type of granite and can cost hundreds of dollars each.

(14) Curling is fun to play and watch, and more people should try it.$q$, 'draft')
