-- Passage wg9-informative-writing-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('513ce2d0-73bf-5ff5-8cbd-357fafc7670d', 'wg9-informative-writing-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g9-informative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Art of Paper Marbling$q$, $q$(1) Some old books have endpapers covered in swirling patterns of color that look almost like polished stone. (2) These designs were made with paper marbling, a craft in which artists float paint on a liquid and then lift the pattern onto paper. (3) Last spring, students in Ms. Okafor's art class at Ridgeview High School spent three weeks learning the technique.

(4) Marbling starts with a shallow tray of size, a liquid thickened with a plant-based powder so that it is slightly thicker than water. (5) The thick liquid allows drops of paint to float on the surface instead of sinking. (6) The artist sprinkles several colors onto the size, and each drop spreads out into a flat circle. (7) Then the artist drags a comb or a thin stick through the colors to pull them into feathers, waves or spirals.

(8) To capture the design, the artist gently lays a sheet of paper on the surface. (9) The paper absorbs the floating paint in a few seconds. (10) After it is lifted and rinsed, the sheet is hung up to dry. (11) Because nearly all of the paint transfers to the paper, each pattern can be printed only once.

(12) Ms. Okafor's students found the process harder than it looked. (13) About one in three of their first sheets came out blurry or streaked. (14) The art room has large windows that face the football field. (15) By the final week, though, most students could produce clean, sharp patterns.$q$, 'draft')
