-- Passage wg9-informative-writing-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('08a08953-92af-50f1-bab5-d03f2a9fb6b4', 'wg9-informative-writing-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g9-informative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$How a Refrigerator Moves Heat$q$, $q$(1) Most people think a refrigerator works by adding cold to the food inside it. (2) Actually, a refrigerator does the opposite: it removes heat from inside the box and releases that heat into the kitchen. (3) The secret is a special fluid called a refrigerant that travels through a closed loop of tubes.

(4) The refrigerant has an unusual property. (5) It boils at a very low temperature, far below the freezing point of water. (6) When the liquid refrigerant flows through coils inside the refrigerator, it boils and turns into a gas. (7) Boiling requires energy, so the refrigerant absorbs heat from the air and food around the coils. (8) This is the same reason sweat cools your skin as it evaporates.

(9) The gas then travels to a pump called a compressor, which squeezes it into a smaller space. (10) Squeezing the gas makes it very hot. (11) The hot gas moves through coils on the back or bottom of the refrigerator, where it gives off its heat to the room and turns back into a liquid. (12) The liquid flows back into the coils inside the box, and the loop starts again. (13) My family's refrigerator is silver and has an ice maker in the door.

(14) Keeping food cold slows the growth of bacteria that cause spoilage. (15) Milk that would sour in a day on the counter can stay fresh for a week or more in a refrigerator set to 4 degrees Celsius.$q$, 'draft')
