        '9-12', $q$Draft: Catching Water from the Air$q$, $q$(1) In his article "Nets That Drink the Fog," water scientist Tomas Arriaga describes how the hillside village of Las Brisas began collecting drinking water from fog. (2) Fog is a cloud that forms close to the ground. (3) Arriaga explains that villagers stretched fine plastic mesh between tall poles on a ridge where fog drifts in from the ocean almost every morning. (4) Tiny droplets cling to the mesh, join together, and run down into a gutter that feeds a storage tank. (5) According to Arriaga, the twelve nets on the ridge collected an average of 2,400 liters of water on foggy days during their first year. (6) This shows that fog nets can turn an ordinary weather pattern into a steady supply of water.

(7) Arriaga also describes how the water changed daily life in Las Brisas. (8) Before the nets were built, students at the village school spent nearly two hours each morning carrying water up from a stream in the valley. (9) ____, many of those students now arrive at class on time and stay for the full school day. (10) Arriaga mentions that the poles were painted bright blue so that pilots could see them. (11) These details suggest that the real value of the nets is not only the water itself but also the time it gives back to families.

(12) In the end, fog is very interesting.$q$, 'draft')
