-- Passage wg9-writing-with-evidence-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('8effa8cb-b877-53d5-b31a-3ceef8d9f4ab', 'wg9-writing-with-evidence-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g9-writing-with-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Draft: A Library on Rails$q$, $q$(1) Historian Ines Kowalczyk's article "The Reading Car" tells the story of a railroad car that worked as a traveling library in the farm towns of the Calder Valley from 1921 to 1934. (2) Kowalczyk shows that the reading car did more than lend books, because it connected isolated towns to the wider world. (3) She explains that most of the valley's towns had no library, and the nearest bookstore was a full day's ride away. (4) ____ (5) This detail shows how eager families were for reading material that their own towns could not provide.

(6) The car also brought news and ideas. (7) Kowalczyk notes that the librarian, a former teacher named Ruth Okonkwo, pinned newspapers from three different cities to a board inside the car. (8) Farmers who came in for seed catalogs often stayed to read about new inventions and events in distant places. (9) The railroad company later replaced its wooden cars with steel ones. (10) Kowalczyk adds that several towns started their own reading clubs after the car's visits, and two of them eventually built permanent libraries. (11) ____, the reading car left behind something that lasted longer than any single visit.

(12) Overall, Kowalczyk's article shows that the reading car opened a door to the world for valley families, and that its effects lasted long after its final trip.$q$, 'draft')
